  <!-- ============================================
       03. THE CASE
       ============================================ -->
  <section class="section case" id="case">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="section-number">03</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">The Case</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="section-body">
      <p class="section-lede">
        Without shared vocabulary, every team teaches their AI the same things in different words, and gets back outputs that drift from how the company actually works. Pace is the fix: codify the way once, use it everywhere.
      </p>

      <ol class="case-list">
        <li class="case-item">
          <h3 class="case-title">One vocabulary, many functions</h3>
          <p>Engineering, product, ops, and marketing share commands instead of inventing private dialects. The agent stops guessing what each team means.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="case-item">
          <h3 class="case-title">Same shape across harnesses</h3>
          <p>Cursor, Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Codex CLI, and 8 more: pace installs the same skill into each. One process, one set of reviews, one company.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="case-item">
          <h3 class="case-title">Defers to specialists</h3>
          <p>Design lives at <a href="https://impeccable.style">impeccable</a>. Pace doesn't compete, it routes. The installer offers impeccable alongside.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="case-item">
          <h3 class="case-title">Codified, not memorized</h3>
          <p>Your way of working stops living in tribal knowledge and starts living in <code>skill/reference/</code>. New hires and new agents pick it up the same way.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="case-item">
          <h3 class="case-title">Ships code and artifacts, not advice</h3>
          <p>Each command produces an actual output: a PR review, a spec doc, a runbook entry. No vague suggestions.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="case-item">
          <h3 class="case-title">Open and forkable</h3>
          <p>Apache 2.0. Use it at GoldenBerry, fork it for your company, contribute commands back.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================
       04. GET STARTED
       ============================================ -->
  <section class="section downloads" id="downloads">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="section-number">04</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Get Started</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="section-body">
      <div class="install-primary">
        <h3 class="install-step"><span class="step-num">1</span>Register the marketplace</h3>
        <p class="install-desc">
          One command registers all 50 plugins with Claude. You only do this once per project.
        </p>

        <div class="terminal">
          <div class="terminal-bar">
            <span class="terminal-dot" data-dot="r"></span>
            <span class="terminal-dot" data-dot="y"></span>
            <span class="terminal-dot" data-dot="g"></span>
            <span class="terminal-label">register</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="terminal-body"><code><span class="prompt">$</span> claude plugin marketplace add GoldenBerry-SO/pace.tools</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="install-primary">
        <h3 class="install-step"><span class="step-num">2</span>Install the plugins you need</h3>
        <p class="install-desc">
          Pick what your team actually uses. A salesperson installs <code>sales</code>. An engineer installs <code>engineering</code> + <code>data</code>. Everyone can install the <code>pace</code> router for GoldenBerry-specific commands.
        </p>

        <div class="terminal">
          <div class="terminal-bar">
            <span class="terminal-dot" data-dot="r"></span>
            <span class="terminal-dot" data-dot="y"></span>
            <span class="terminal-dot" data-dot="g"></span>
            <span class="terminal-label">install</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="terminal-body"><code><span class="prompt">$</span> claude plugin install pace@pace.tools          <span class="muted"># company router</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> claude plugin install sales@pace.tools         <span class="muted"># /sales:* commands</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> claude plugin install engineering@pace.tools   <span class="muted"># /engineering:* commands</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> claude plugin install data@pace.tools          <span class="muted"># /data:* commands</span></code></pre>
        </div>

        <p class="install-note">
          Browse the full catalog at <a href="/plugins">/plugins</a>. For frontend code design, install <a href="https://impeccable.style"><strong>impeccable</strong></a> alongside.
        </p>

        <details class="install-alt">
          <summary>Or use the pace CLI for a guided picker</summary>
          <div class="install-alt-body">
            <p>The pace CLI wraps the marketplace flow with an interactive prompt:</p>
            <div class="terminal terminal-compact">
              <pre class="terminal-body"><code><span class="prompt">$</span> npx pace skills install</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p>The picker installs the pace router and offers to bring impeccable along for design.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <div class="install-followup">
        <h3 class="install-step"><span class="step-num">3</span>Configure connectors (optional)</h3>
        <p class="install-desc">
          Plugins like <code>sales</code>, <code>data</code>, <code>marketing</code> ship with <code>.mcp.json</code> for connectors (Slack, HubSpot, Snowflake, etc.). Most work without them; connecting unlocks the supercharged version.
        </p>

        <h3 class="install-step"><span class="step-num">4</span>Use the commands</h3>
        <p class="install-desc">
          Type <code>/pace &lt;cmd&gt;</code> for the GoldenBerry router. Type <code>/sales:call-prep</code>, <code>/data:write-query</code>, <code>/marketing:campaign-plan</code> for namespaced department commands. Skills also auto-trigger from natural language, so <em>"prep me for my call with Acme"</em> finds <code>/sales:call-prep</code> on its own.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================
       05. WHAT'S NEW
       ============================================ -->
  <section class="section changelog" id="changelog">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="section-number">05</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">What's New</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="section-body">
      <ul class="changelog-items">
        <li class="changelog-entry">
          <div class="changelog-meta">
            <span class="changelog-version">v0.2.0</span>
            <span class="changelog-date">May 2026</span>
          </div>
          <div class="changelog-content">
            <h3>Marketplace pivot</h3>
            <p>Pace becomes a curated Claude Code marketplace. Imports <a href="https://github.com/anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins">Anthropic's knowledge-work-plugins</a> verbatim (17 first-party + 5 partner-built), references 27 external plugins by git URL, and keeps the <code>/pace</code> router as our company-specific layer on top. New <code>/plugins</code> page lists the full catalog with attribution per plugin.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="changelog-entry">
          <div class="changelog-meta">
            <span class="changelog-version">v0.1.0</span>
            <span class="changelog-date">May 2026</span>
          </div>
          <div class="changelog-content">
            <h3>Initial scaffold</h3>
            <p>The pace skill router lands as a lean fork of impeccable's install machinery: multi-harness build, Astro site shell, CLI installer with impeccable handoff. Router starts empty; commands ship as they're authored.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="changelog-footnote">
        Track work in progress on <a href="https://github.com/GoldenBerry-SO/pace.tools" class="text-link">GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================
       06. FAQ
       ============================================ -->
  <section class="section faq" id="faq">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="section-number">06</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="section-body">
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is pace, exactly?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Pace is two things in one repo:</p>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>A Claude Code marketplace</strong> that registers 50 plugins: <a href="https://github.com/anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins">Anthropic's knowledge-work-plugins</a> (sales, marketing, engineering, data, finance, legal, etc.) imported verbatim, 5 partner-built plugins, and 27 external plugins referenced by git URL.</li>
              <li><strong>A company router</strong> (<code>/pace</code>) for GoldenBerry-specific commands. Empty in v0.2; we add as we author.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>One marketplace URL gives Claude visibility to everything. Pick which plugins to install per project or per person.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How is pace different from impeccable?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p><strong>Impeccable</strong> is a single design skill focused entirely on frontend code craft (typography, color, layout, motion at the CSS/component level).</p>
            <p><strong>Pace</strong> is a marketplace covering every other knowledge-work domain plus a company router. Anthropic's <code>design</code> plugin also lives in pace, but that's about UX research / dev handoff / accessibility audits, not code-level CSS.</p>
            <p>Most projects want both. The pace CLI offers to install impeccable alongside.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why import Anthropic's plugins verbatim instead of writing our own?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Anthropic's <a href="https://github.com/anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins">knowledge-work-plugins</a> are Apache 2.0, well-researched, and already cover most knowledge-work scenarios. Reinventing them would be slower and probably worse.</p>
            <p>By importing verbatim, we get:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Upstream sync</strong>: Anthropic ships an improvement, we re-sync and ship it too.</li>
              <li><strong>No drift</strong>: we don't accidentally diverge from a well-curated baseline.</li>
              <li><strong>A clean customization story</strong>: when GoldenBerry needs to customize a plugin, we fork it to a new name (<code>plugins/sales-gb/</code>) rather than editing the imported one.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Can I install just one plugin?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. After registering the marketplace, you install plugins individually:</p>
            <pre><code>claude plugin marketplace add GoldenBerry-SO/pace.tools
claude plugin install sales@pace.tools</code></pre>
            <p>That's the whole flow. Install <code>pace</code> for the company router, <code>sales</code> for sales commands, <code>data</code> for SQL workflows, etc. The plugins don't depend on each other.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How do MCP connectors work?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Plugins like <code>sales</code>, <code>marketing</code>, <code>data</code>, <code>productivity</code> ship with a <code>.mcp.json</code> declaring the MCP servers they integrate with (Slack, HubSpot, Snowflake, Notion, etc.).</p>
            <p>Claude prompts you to authorize each connector the first time a skill needs it. You don't need to install or configure anything manually. Plugins still work without their connectors; skills degrade gracefully to "tell me about your account, I'll work with what you give me."</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What if I want to customize a plugin for our company?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Don't edit the imported plugin under <code>plugins/&lt;name&gt;/</code> directly: that forks you from upstream and breaks sync.</p>
            <p>Instead: copy the plugin to a new directory (e.g., <code>plugins/sales-gb/</code>), update its <code>plugin.json</code> name + author, and edit there. Add an entry to <code>marketplace.json</code> pointing at the new path. Users opt into your fork explicitly.</p>
            <p>For company-specific commands that don't fit any imported plugin, add them to the <code>/pace</code> router under <code>skill/reference/&lt;cmd&gt;.md</code>. That's what the router exists for.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What commands does the /pace router ship?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>None yet (v0.2 is the marketplace pivot; the router still starts empty). Commands ship to the router as we author them.</p>
            <p>Authoring a router command: drop a reference file at <code>skill/reference/&lt;cmd&gt;.md</code>, register it in <code>skill/SKILL.md</code> and <code>skill/scripts/command-metadata.json</code>, run <code>bun run build</code>, ship a PR. See <a href="/docs" class="text-link">/docs</a> for the full guide.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How do I update to the latest plugins?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>From any installed project:</p>
            <pre><code>claude plugin update --all</code></pre>
            <p>Pace's CLI also exposes <code>npx pace skills update</code> which updates the <code>/pace</code> router specifically.</p>
            <p>Pace itself periodically re-syncs from Anthropic's upstream repo. New plugins and updated SKILL.md files land in our marketplace as a normal pace release.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Can I pin <code>/pace audit</code> to just <code>/audit</code>?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. Pace inherits impeccable's pin mechanism: <code>node skill/scripts/pin.mjs pin &lt;command&gt;</code> creates a lightweight redirect so <code>/audit</code> delegates to <code>/pace audit</code>.</p>
            <p>Available once the command is in the allowlist; the allowlist is empty in v0.2.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Is pace open source?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. Apache 2.0 throughout. Pace's original code (router + marketplace) is GoldenBerry's; the imported plugins remain attributed to Anthropic and the partner authors. See <a href="https://github.com/GoldenBerry-SO/pace.tools/blob/main/NOTICE.md" class="text-link">NOTICE.md</a> for full attribution.</p>
            <p>Fork it for your company; the marketplace shape and install machinery carry over for free.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================
       07. USE PACE AT YOUR COMPANY
       ============================================ -->
  <section class="section company-cta" id="adopt">
